>Much more is possible in non-wizmode games, I think, than you believe
>possible.  It's possible, though tedious, to get more than a thousand
>hit points, and with the right armor and polymorphing, an AC of -75
>isn't too hard to manage.

Armour cannot safely be enchanted above when it's above +3 (+5 for
elven armour), so the maximum enchantment you can safely get is +5 for
normal non-elven armour. Assuming you have all seven layers of armour,
and have some fifty blessed scrolls of enchant armour, it might be
possible to keep enchanting until you get it to all +6.

Now the best I can think of (in terms of AC) is:

Dragon scale mail (9), cloak of protection (3), dwarvish iron helm (2),
any gauntlets or gloves (1), any good shield (2), any good boots (2),
and an Hawaiian shirt (0). Add seven times 6 to this, and you get an AC
of 10-61 = -51. This is by far not -75 yet. You must really have played
in wizard mode too much.

Of course, you can eat the appropriate rings (of protection) a lot,
donate money to a priest, and wear some rings of protection as well,
which might lower your AC some more. You wouldn't want to do this,
though, since it will greatly increase your food consumption,
permanently in the case of eating rings.


